"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[3129],{43129:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"schemaVersion":1,"locale":"en","slug":"01-20-let","title":"Lesson 01. 20 Years Against Inflation: What Preserved Capital and What Depreciated","sourceFingerprint":"189132a2186bc87f944935284453b9e878ade1adf3b4ae3332c35d5e9c45492f","blocks":[{"id":"b001","markdown":"## Chapter 1. A 20-Year Distance: Who Outran the Ruble\'s Depreciation and Who Lost Ground"},{"id":"b002","markdown":"> \\"Time ruthlessly exposes the truth: an asset that seems an unshakable rock over a monthly horizon can quietly dissolve a third of your money\'s purchasing power across a two-decade distance.\\""},{"id":"b003","markdown":"In the first volume of the workbook, we examined in detail how inflation erodes the purchasing power of banknotes hidden under a mattress. It is time to step up and look at the broader picture: what would have happened to the savings of a person who distributed their starting capital across various instruments twenty years ago and never touched it once?"},{"id":"b004","markdown":"Financial market analysts have summarized the results of the twenty-year marathon from August 2006 to August 2026. Below are the average annual return rates of key investment avenues within Russian jurisdiction."},{"id":"b005","markdown":"### Results of the Twenty-Year Return Race (August 2006 – August 2026)"},{"id":"b006","markdown":"| Where funds were allocated | Average annual growth | Real performance relative to inflation |\\n| :--- | :---: | :--- |\\n| **Gold (physical metal)** | **16.9%** | Confidently outpaced price increases |\\n| **New build apartments (square meter only)** | **10.2%** | Steady growth in purchasing power |\\n| **Bank deposits up to 12 months** | **9.4%** | Slight net gain over inflation |\\n| **Federal Loan Bonds (OFZ)** | **8.0%** | Minimal protection on the verge of breaking even |\\n| **Secondary residential real estate (square meter only)** | **8.0%** | On the brink of value preservation |\\n| **Official inflation rate (Rosstat)** | **7.8%** | **Baseline benchmark for capital survival** |\\n| **Moscow Exchange Equity Index (total return, incl. dividends)** | **7.5%** | Below the breakeven threshold as of the measurement date |\\n| **Cash US Dollar** | **6.0%** | Systematic loss of purchasing power |"},{"id":"b007","markdown":"## Debriefing: 5 Lessons of the Distance"},{"id":"b008","markdown":"### 1. Popular \\"safe havens\\" do not guarantee a win\\nThe main surprise for a conservative saver is foreign currency. It is commonly assumed that buying US dollars protects against any economic storm. However, the figures are uncompromising: the ruble return on foreign currency over two decades was only 6% per annum versus inflation of 7.8%. Savings kept in dollars under the pillow \\"for a rainy day\\" lost almost a third of their real value domestically."},{"id":"b009","markdown":"### 2. A bank deposit is a reliable reservoir, not a wealth generator\\nShort-term deposits delivered 9.4% per annum, outpacing Rosstat by a modest 1.5% in net real returns.\\n- For building an emergency fund or saving for a vacation, this is a flawless choice: accessible, liquid, insured by the DIA.\\n- For creating a lifetime annuity in 20–25 years, a deposit is fundamentally insufficient: capital simply treads water, barely adding any purchasing power."},{"id":"b010","markdown":"### 3. The measurement date trap: why you cannot trust a chart with a single finish point\\nThe Russian stock market showed 7.5% per annum, formally lagging inflation. Does this mean equity investing has failed? Far from it. The final calculation point (August 2026) coincided with a deep cyclical market correction."},{"id":"b011","markdown":"Consider this comparison: if analysts had recorded the 20-year interval just a year and a half earlier (in January 2025), the average return on equities would have stood at an impressive **13.6% per year**. The exact same asset class, the exact same holdings, yet shifting the end date leads to the exact opposite conclusion."},{"id":"b012","markdown":"> **Investor marker:** when presented with \\"proven historical outperformance,\\" always check between which specific dates the measurement was taken. Starting during a crisis and ending at an all-time peak will create the illusion of wealth even where none exists."},{"id":"b013","markdown":"### 4. Past favorites can disappoint over the short run\\nGold took first place in the table with an outstanding 16.9% per annum. However, the asset\'s behavior within any single year is fraught with stress.\\n- **January 27, 2026:** the Bank of Russia (CBR) official accounting price hits a record high of 12,088 ₽ per gram.\\n- **October 24, 2026:** the quote pulls back to 10,482 ₽ per gram (a 13% drop in just 9 months).\\n- **Early November 2026:** the quote hovers near 10,618 ₽."},{"id":"b014","markdown":"Anyone who succumbed to the hype and entered the precious metal at the January peak, guided by the victories of the past 20 years, found themselves in a severe temporary drawdown."},{"id":"b015","markdown":"### 5. The real return on real estate is hidden in the details\\nSquare meter price growth statistics (10.2% on the primary market and 8% on the secondary market) reflect solely the price of the walls themselves. Renting out residential property potentially adds around 6% in gross yield per year."},{"id":"b016","markdown":"However, the real net rental yield is substantially lower: income tax, ongoing repairs, tenant vacancy periods, utility costs, and your personal time spent managing the property are inevitably subtracted from it."},{"id":"b017","markdown":"## \uD83E\uDDEE Margin Calculation: The \\"Green Stash\\" Syndrome"},{"id":"b018","markdown":"Consider an investor who in August 2006 converted **100,000 ₽** into cash foreign currency at an effective 6% annual return and opened the envelope exactly 20 years later:"},{"id":"b019","markdown":"1. **Actual amount on hand by August 2026:**\\n   $$100\\\\,000 \\\\times (1 + 0{,}06)^{20} \\\\approx 320\\\\,714 \\\\text{ ₽}$$"},{"id":"b020","markdown":"2. **Amount required to preserve original purchasing power (inflation of 7.8% per annum):**\\n   $$100\\\\,000 \\\\times (1 + 0{,}078)^{20} \\\\approx 449\\\\,133 \\\\text{ ₽}$$"},{"id":"b021","markdown":"3. **Depreciation math:**\\n   $$\\\\frac{449\\\\,133 - 320\\\\,714}{449\\\\,133} \\\\approx 28{,}6\\\\% \\\\approx 29\\\\%$$"},{"id":"b022","markdown":"**The 20-year result:** nominally, the ruble equivalent grew more than threefold, but the real basket of goods that can be purchased with these funds has **contracted by 29%**."},{"id":"b023","markdown":"## Personal Price Index: Why Other People\'s Statistics Lie"},{"id":"b024","markdown":"The official Rosstat indicator (7.8%) is calculated based on a broad, averaged consumer basket of a typical resident. However, personal inflation is always individual:\\n- a family with three children experiences price hikes on extracurriculars, children\'s clothing, and tutors;\\n- a car owner responds to price trends in spare parts, compulsory motor third-party liability insurance (OSAGO), and fuel;\\n- a tenant is tied to rental rates in their specific neighborhood."},{"id":"b025","markdown":"If your primary expenditure categories rose by 12–14% per year, even instruments yielding above official inflation failed to allow your capital to grow in real terms."},{"id":"b026","markdown":"## \uD83D\uDCCC Key Principles of Protecting Savings"},{"id":"b027","markdown":"1. **There is no universal ideal asset in nature.** Every instrument goes through cycles of booms and prolonged downturns. Attempting to bet on a single favorite is a direct path to uncontrolled risks.\\n2. **There is no free protection against inflation.** An investor pays for returns above the inflation rate with volatility — a willingness to endure temporary balance drawdowns of tens of percent.\\n3. **Smart diversification is the only free lunch in the market.** A balance between defensive conservative instruments (LQDT money market funds, deposits, short OFZ) and long-term growth instruments (stocks, PAXG gold/metal) protects capital from any local crisis."}],"terms":[{"id":"realnaya-dohodnost","term":"Real Return","definition":"the nominal return on an asset minus the rate of inflation."},{"id":"zoloto","term":"Investment Gold","definition":"a safe-haven asset (bullion, GLDRUB, #PAXG) that preserves purchasing power."},{"id":"indeks-mosbirzhi","term":"MOEX Index","definition":"a price indicator tracking the shares of the largest Russian companies."}],"quizzes":[{"id":"q1","q":"An investor kept ruble capital in cash US dollars for 20 years. The average currency appreciation rate was 6% per year, while average inflation was 7.8%. What happened to their purchasing power?","options":["They locked in a real profit, as the dollar exchange rate always strengthens over the long run.","Purchasing power fell by nearly a third, as the currency\'s ruble return failed to keep pace with domestic price growth.","Capital increased by exactly 1.8% in net annual return."],"correct":1,"explanation":"When an asset\'s return (6%) is lower than the ruble\'s depreciation rate (7.8%), the real value of capital melts away: by 2026, the accumulated amount buys 29% fewer goods and services than in 2006."},{"id":"q2","q":"Why was the 20-year average annualized return of the MOEX stock index 13.6% when measured at the beginning of 2025, but dropped to 7.5% per annum when calculated in August 2026?","options":["Analysts excluded the dividends received by investors from the calculation.","The outcome of any long-term measurement directly depends on the market\'s current phase at the end point: August 2026 coincided with a market downturn.","In a year and a half, Russian companies lost all their profits from the preceding two decades."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Historical performance is sensitive to the end measurement date. Having the final data point land during a market correction sharply reduces the calculated annualized return, even over a 20-year horizon."},{"id":"q3","q":"Gold demonstrated the best 20-year result (16.9% per annum). Does this mean that buying physical metal or digital gold guarantees a profit over any timeframe?","options":["Yes, precious metals are fundamentally protected from price corrections.","No, over shorter intervals even the top performer experiences declines: for example, over 9 months in 2026, the price of a gram of gold dropped 13% from its January peak.","Profit is guaranteed only when purchasing highest-grade bullion bars."],"correct":1,"explanation":"High long-term returns are accompanied by price volatility. Buying an asset at the height of emotional hype can keep a position in the red for months or years, despite strong historical track records."}],"officialSources":[]}')}}]);